Node Affinity attracts Pods to specific Nodes, 
while Taints and Tolerations repel Pods from Nodes unless explicitly allowed.

NODE AFFINITY (Attraction)                      TAINTS & TOLERATIONS (Repulsion)
  Pod has preferences/requirements.                 Node repels all pods by default.

  [ Pod: "I want a GPU node" ]                      [ Pod without pass ]
               |                                             |
               v (Attracted)                                 x (Repelled by Taint)
  +--------------------------+                      +--------------------------+
  | Node: disk=ssd, gpu=true |                      | Node: dedicated=gpu      |
  +--------------------------+                      | Taint: NoSchedule        |
                                                    +--------------------------+
                                                             ^
                                                             | (Allowed through)
                                                    [ Pod with Toleration:     ]
                                                    [ "I tolerate dedicated=gpu"]


Core Concepts Explained

1. Node Affinity: Pod-Driven Placement

Node Affinity lets you specify rules matching labels on nodes. It replaces the older, 
simpler nodeSelector.

requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution (Hard Rule): The scheduler must find a node matching this rule.
 If no matching node exists, the Pod remains Pending.

preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution (Soft Rule): The scheduler tries to satisfy the rule, but if
 no matching node exists, it places the Pod on any available node.

IgnoredDuringExecution: If node labels change after the Pod is already running, the Pod will continue
 running without being evicted.


 2. Taints: Node-Driven Repulsion
A taint is set on a Node using the syntax key=value:effect. It creates an exclusion zone:

NoSchedule: New Pods lacking a matching toleration will never be scheduled on this node. 
 Existing running Pods remain unaffected.

PreferNoSchedule: A soft warning. The scheduler attempts to avoid placing untolerating Pods on this node,
 but will use it if the cluster is out of capacity elsewhere.

NoExecute: Eviction rule. New Pods without a matching toleration are blocked, and already-running Pods 
on the node that lack the toleration are immediately terminated and evicted.

3. Tolerations: The Pod's Entry Pass
A toleration is declared under spec.tolerations in the Pod manifest. It allows (but does not force) 
a Pod to schedule onto a tainted node.

Production Pattern: Taints and Node Affinity are frequently paired together.

The Taint prevents unwanted general workloads from landing on expensive or sensitive 
nodes (e.g., GPU, payment PCI-DSS).

The Node Affinity ensures the intended workload actively targets those dedicated nodes rather
 than landing on standard general-purpose nodes.





